The '''2004 Football League Cup final''' (known as the [[Carling]] Cup final for sponsorship reasons) was a football match that took place on 29 February 2004 at the [[Millennium Stadium]], [[Cardiff]]. It was the final match of the [[2003–04 Football League Cup]], the 44th edition of the [[EFL Cup|Football League Cup]], a competition for the 92 teams in the [[Premier League]] and [[English Football League|The Football League]].

It was contested between Premier League sides [[Bolton Wanderers F.C.|Bolton Wanderers]] and [[Middlesbrough F.C.|Middlesbrough]], both of whom had never won in their previous appearances in the final. Bolton's run to the final included a win away to holders [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in the fourth round, while requiring extra time against [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] in the next round and losing the second leg of the semi-final against [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]]. Middlesbrough needed extra time against [[Brighton & Hove Albion]] in their first game, and later advanced on [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-outs]] against top-flight [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] and [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]]; they then won home and away against league leaders [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] in the semi-finals.

Middlesbrough scored two goals in the first seven minutes, from [[Joseph-Désiré Job]] and a [[Boudewijn Zenden]] penalty. [[Kevin Davies]] got a goal back in the first half after an error by Boro goalkeeper [[Mark Schwarzer]], but Middlesbrough kept the score at 2–1 for their first major trophy. In doing so, they qualified for the [[2004–05 UEFA Cup]], their first European competition.

==Background==
Bolton were playing in their second League Cup final, having lost the [[1995 Football League Cup final|1995]] edition 2–1 to [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]]. Middlesbrough too had never previously won the competition: in [[1997 Football League Cup final|1997]] they lost the final after extra time in a replay against [[Leicester City]], and a year later they lost 2–0 to [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] after extra time.<ref>{{cite web|title=England - Football League Cup Finals |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/engleagcuphist.html |access-date=1 April 2016 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160329230552/http://www.rsssf.com/tablese/engleagcuphist.html |archive-date=29 March 2016 |website=[[RSSSF]] |df=dmy }}</ref> In 1997, they also lost the [[FA Cup]] [[1997 FA Cup final|final]] to Chelsea, and [[1996–97 Premier League|were relegated]] from the Premier League.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Saleem|first1=Omar|title=How Middlesbrough's mid-1990s transfers changed English football|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/these-football-times/2014/jul/31/middlesbrough-transfers-changed-english-football-juninho-emerson-ravanelli|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=31 July 2004}}</ref> Although Middlesbrough, founded in 1876, were without a major trophy, Bolton had 4 FA Cups from 7 finals between [[1894 FA Cup final|1894]] and [[1958 FA Cup final|1958]].<ref>{{cite web|title=England FA Challenge Cup Finals|website=[[RSSSF]]|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/engcuphist.html|access-date=1 April 2016}}</ref>

Bolton and Middlesbrough had already met once in the [[2003–04 Premier League|league season]], at the former's [[Reebok Stadium]] on 13 September 2003. The hosts won 2–0 with goals by [[Bruno Ngotty]] and [[Kevin Davies]] in each half for a first victory of the campaign.<ref>{{cite news|title=Bolton seal first win|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/3194205.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=13 September 2003}}</ref>

