As a Premier League team, Bolton began in the second round, hosting Walsall of the [[Football League First Division|First Division]] at the Reebok Stadium. They won 3–1 with two goals – one a penalty kick – by Brazilian debutant [[Mário Jardel]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Bolton 3-1 Walsall|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3125074.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=24 September 2003}}</ref> In the next round on 28 October, they again hosted a second-tier team, this time Gillingham, and won 2–0 with goals by [[Stelios Giannakopoulos]] and [[Henrik Pedersen (footballer)|Henrik Pedersen]], in front of a notably low crowd of 5,258.<ref>{{cite news|title=Bolton 2-0 Gillingham|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3204809.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=28 October 2003}}</ref>

In the fourth round, Bolton had their first trip, and their first game against top-flight opposition, facing defending champions [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] at [[Anfield]] on 3 December. Jardel scored the first goal within five minutes, with [[Danny Murphy (footballer, born 1977)|Danny Murphy]] equalising with 20 minutes remaining. [[Youri Djorkaeff]] would have put Bolton back into the lead, but referee [[Mike Riley (referee)|Mike Riley]] disallowed it for an earlier handball; they scored minutes later from [[Jay-Jay Okocha]]'s free kick. Liverpool got a second equaliser from a 25-yard [[Vladimír Šmicer]] strike, but with a minute remaining [[Salif Diao]] fouled Kevin Davies in the penalty area and Djorkaeff sent the resulting spot-kick past Liverpool goalkeeper [[Jerzy Dudek]] to win the game.<ref>{{cite news|title=Bolton stun Liverpool|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3247008.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=3 December 2003}}</ref> Thirteen days later, in extra time at home to the Premier League's [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]], Pedersen volleyed the only goal with five minutes remaining.<ref>{{cite news|title=Bolton see off Saints|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3314295.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=16 December 2003}}</ref>

On 21 January 2004, Bolton hosted [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] in the first leg of an all-Premier League semi-final. Okocha gave them the lead with a second-minute free kick, and further strikes from [[Kevin Nolan]] and Stelios gave them a 3–0 lead after 17 minutes, with [[Juan Pablo Ángel]] getting one back for the visitors soon after. Ángel got another goal in the second half, before Ngotty's header and a second Okocha free kick gave Bolton a 5–2 victory.<ref>{{cite news|title=Bolton 5-2 Aston Villa|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3412549.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=21 January 2004}}</ref> Six days later in the second leg at [[Villa Park]], Bolton lost 2–0 to a Villa team who had [[Gavin McCann]] sent off in the first half, but advanced to the final 5–4 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|title=Aston Villa 2-0 Bolton|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3417689.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=27 January 2004}}</ref>

