Middlesbrough, also of the Premier League, began the competition in the second round by hosting [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton Hove Albion]] at the [[Riverside Stadium]], and defeated the [[Football League Second Division|Second Division]] team 1–0 with a goal from [[Malcolm Christie]] at the start of extra time.<ref>{{cite news|title=Middlesbrough 1-0 Brighton|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3125084.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=24 September 2003}}</ref> In the next round, they travelled to [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]] of the First Division and won 2–1 with [[Massimo Maccarone]] and [[Gaizka Mendieta]]'s first goals of the season, despite the hosts at the [[DW Stadium|JJB Stadium]] scoring a late free kick through [[Jimmy Bullard]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Wigan 1-2 Middlesbrough|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3204855.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=28 October 2003}}</ref>

They met their first top-flight opposition in the fourth round, hosting [[Everton F.C.|Everton]]. After 120 goalless minutes, the match went to a [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] in which Mendieta scored the winner after Middlesbrough goalkeeper [[Mark Schwarzer]] saved from [[Leon Osman]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Boro through on penalties|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3247018.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=3 December 2003}}</ref> The quarter-final at another Premier League team, [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]], also went to a shoot-out. [[Darren Anderton]] scored for the hosts within 63 seconds, the first goal Boro conceded for over 11 hours. With four minutes to play, Middlesbrough striker [[Michael Ricketts]] equalised after a cross from [[George Boateng]]. The shoot-out, in which Schwarzer denied [[Mauricio Taricco]] and [[Kasey Keller]] blocked from Mendieta, went to sudden death, in which Middlesbrough's goalkeeper saved from [[Gus Poyet]] and [[Franck Queudrue]] scored Boro's winner.<ref>{{cite news|title=Spurs suffer penalty agony|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3314311.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=17 December 2003}}</ref>

In the semi-finals, Boro faced [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]], the leaders and [[2003–04 Arsenal F.C. season|eventual unbeaten champions]] of the Premier League season. In the first leg at [[Highbury Stadium|Highbury]] on 20 January 2004, Queudrue exploited a mistake by Arsenal defender [[Martin Keown]] and passed to Mendieta, who then assisted [[Juninho Paulista|Juninho]] for the only goal of the game.<ref>{{cite news|title=Arsenal 0-1 Middlesbrough|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3403993.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=20 January 2004}}</ref> On 3 February, Boro hosted Arsenal in the second leg; Keown was sent off at the end of the first half for a [[professional foul]] on Maccarone. In the second half, [[Boudewijn Zenden]] put Middlesbrough ahead and [[Edu (footballer, born 1978)|Edu]] equalised. With five minutes remaining, Arsenal's [[José Antonio Reyes]] scored an [[own goal]] by deflecting [[Stuart Parnaby]]'s shot, making Middlesbrough advance to the final 3–1 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|title=Middlesbrough 2-1 Arsenal|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3451565.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=3 February 2004}}</ref>

==Match==
===Pre-match===
Bolton manager [[Sam Allardyce]], who was building a reputation for signing unwanted foreign veterans such as Ngotty, Djorkaeff and [[Iván Campo]], predicted that a win would help him sign more players in the summer. However, he thought that success would also lead his 13 players who would be out of contract, to demand more money for a renewal.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Johnson|first1=William|title=Djorkaeff delighted|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2373900/Djorkaeff-delighted.html|access-date=1 April 2016|work=The Daily Telegraph|date=28 February 2004}}</ref>

On-loan Middlesbrough right-back [[Danny Mills]], a self-acknowledged "hate figure" from opposition fans due to his physical style of play, declared that he would ignore any abuse from Bolton fans.<ref>{{cite news|title=Hate figure loves to win|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2373903/Hate-figure-loves-to-win.html|access-date=1 April 2016|work=The Daily Telegraph}}</ref>

===Summary===
[[File:Zenden.jpg|thumb|[[Boudewijn Zenden]] (pictured playing for Liverpool in 2005) assisted Middlesbrough's first goal and scored their second. He received the [[Alan Hardaker Trophy]] for the final's [[man of the match]].]]
Middlesbrough manager [[Steve McClaren]] had not even sat down when his team took the lead.<ref name=sat>{{cite news|title=Boro lift Carling Cup|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/3507795.stm|access-date=1 April 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=29 February 2004}}</ref> Mendieta's pass let Zenden run down the left wing and cross for striker [[Joseph-Désiré Job]] to put Middlesbrough ahead in the second minute.<ref name=sat/> It was the fastest goal scored in a final, a record broken [[2005 Football League Cup final|the following year]] by Liverpool's [[John Arne Riise]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Stoke City: Middlesbrough in profile|url=http://www.stokesentinel.co.uk/Stoke-City-Middlesbrough-profile/story-12501020-detail/story.html|access-date=1 April 2016|work=Stoke Sentinel|date=30 August 2008}}{{Dead link|date=September 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

After Job's early goal, Bolton went on the attack, with Djorkaeff forcing Schwarzer to make a near-post save.<ref name=sat/> In the seventh minute however, Middlesbrough won a penalty when Job was tripped in the Bolton box by [[Emerson Thome]].<ref name=sat/> Zenden stepped up to take the spot kick and beat Bolton goalkeeper [[Jussi Jääskeläinen]], despite slipping and the goalkeeper's foot touching the ball.<ref name=sat/> Replays showed that Zenden kicked the ball twice as he slipped. Later, Thome nearly scored an own goal from Juninho's cross.<ref name=sat/>

Davies scored for Bolton on 21 minutes when an error by Middlesbrough goalkeeper Schwarzer allowed his shot to go inside his near post. Bolton continued to attack, and Schwarzer made a double save from winger [[Per Frandsen]] and Djorkaeff. The latter had two more chances soon after, missing the target with the first. Boro pleaded for a second penalty when [[Nicky Hunt]] pulled Job, but referee Riley did not give it.<ref name=sat/>

The second half had fewer incidents.<ref name=sat/> Schwarzer saved a header from Nolan, while Juninho had two opportunities for Middlesbrough.<ref name=sat/> In the final few minutes, Thome made a crucial block against Mendieta, and at the other side of the pitch [[Ugo Ehiogu]] did the same to thwart Stelios.<ref name=sat/>

